# Parseltongue Core: Engine Under the Microscope

The parseltongue engine is the heart of the system — it evaluates S-expressions, derives theorems, checks consistency, and catches drift. This notebook loads the engine's own formal specification and inspects it using the workbench.

```scheme
;; pltg Load
(import (quote ....validation.core core))
```

## The Engine's Shape

```scheme
;; pltg Counts
(derive engine-fact-count (std.counting.count-exists
  engine.consistency-checks-three-layers
  engine.consistency-doc
  engine.derive-resolves-definition
  engine.diff-evaluated-structurally
  engine.fact-stores-evidence)
  :using (std.counting.count-exists-base std.counting.count-exists-step
    engine.consistency-checks-three-layers engine.consistency-doc
    engine.derive-resolves-definition engine.diff-evaluated-structurally
    engine.fact-stores-evidence))
engine-fact-count
```

The engine module has [[term:engine-fact-count]] key facts shown here — each one quoting verbatim source code from `engine.py`. [[fact:engine.consistency-checks-three-layers]] describes the three-layer screening: evidence grounding, fabrication propagation, and diff divergences. [[fact:engine.consistency-doc]] records the docstring.

## Consistency — The Engine Checks Itself

The consistency checker runs [[term:engine.consistency-layer-count]] layers of validation:

- **Layer 1**: [[fact:engine.consistency-layer-1]] — evidence grounding. Every fact must cite a real quote.
- **Layer 2**: [[fact:engine.consistency-layer-2]] — fabrication propagation. Theorems derived from ungrounded facts are flagged.
- **Layer 3**: [[fact:engine.consistency-layer-3]] — diff divergences. Stated counts must match reality.

```scheme
;; pltg Consistency Layers
engine.consistency-layer-count
```

## Error Classification

The engine classifies issues into errors and warnings. [[fact:engine.unverified-causes-error]] — unverified evidence is an error, not a warning. [[fact:engine.manual-causes-warning]] — manually verified items get a warning (trust but verify). [[fact:engine.contamination-causes-warning]] — diff contamination (two diffs sharing a replace side) is a warning, not a failure.

```scheme
;; pltg Error Witnesses
(derive error-witness-count (std.counting.count-exists
  engine.witness-evidence-hallucinated
  engine.witness-no-evidence-hallucinated
  engine.witness-fabrication-does-not-hold
  engine.witness-fabrication-ungrounded-source
  engine.witness-evidence-unknown)
  :using (std.counting.count-exists-base std.counting.count-exists-step
    engine.witness-evidence-hallucinated engine.witness-no-evidence-hallucinated
    engine.witness-fabrication-does-not-hold engine.witness-fabrication-ungrounded-source
    engine.witness-evidence-unknown))
error-witness-count
```

[[term:error-witness-count]] witness patterns classify how things fail: hallucinated evidence[[~term:engine.witness-evidence-hallucinated]], missing evidence[[~term:engine.witness-no-evidence-hallucinated]], axiom that doesn't hold[[~term:engine.witness-fabrication-does-not-hold]], ungrounded derivation source[[~term:engine.witness-fabrication-ungrounded-source]], and unknown evidence status[[~term:engine.witness-evidence-unknown]].

## Diff Mechanism — Catching Drift

Diffs compare two values and detect divergence. [[fact:engine.diff-evaluated-structurally]] shows how diffs are evaluated — structurally, not by string comparison.

```scheme
;; pltg Diff Coverage
engine.diff-mechanism-doc-count
```

The engine documents [[term:engine.diff-mechanism-doc-count]] aspects of the diff mechanism. When a diff finds divergence, [[fact:engine.divergence-causes-error]] — it's a hard error. When contamination is detected (shared replace side), [[fact:engine.contamination-causes-warning]] — it's downgraded to a warning.

